@inproceedings{he2026disentangling,
    title = "Disentangling Representation Evolution in Transformers through Directional Decomposition",
    author = "He, Shwai  and
      Zhang, Haichao  and
      Yan, Shen",
    editor = "Lee, Hung-Yi  and
      Dev, Sunipa  and
      Li, Jessy  and
      Elliott, Desmond",
    booktitle = "Findings of the {A}ssociation for {C}omputational {L}inguistics: {EMNLP} 2026",
    month = oct,
    year = "2026",
    address = "Budapest, Hungary",
    publisher = "Association for Computational Linguistics",
    url = "https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1397/",
    doi = "10.18653/2026.findings-emnlp.1397",
    pages = "29759--29772",
    ISBN = "979-8-89176-462-0",
    abstract = "Transformer layers evolve representations through learned additive transformations that combine direction-preserving and direction-changing effects. We study this geometry by decomposing learned updates into parallel and perpendicular components. Across pretrained models, we find substantial learned parallel components, showing that direction-preserving behavior is not confined to the residual identity path. To test whether these components are redundant or behaviorally important, we apply the decomposition to residual-space updates and to value aggregation inside attention. Inference-time edits reveal a space-dependent asymmetry: value-space parallel edits preserve behavior more effectively than residual-space or perpendicular counterparts, especially when the direct self message is fixed and only the non-self aggregate is scaled. The decomposition also provides a compact, component-resolved view of compression error. During from-scratch pretraining, using parallel suppression as part of the forward computation lowers validation-loss trajectories and improves downstream averages in our evaluated runs, with the value-space variant strongest. Overall, this geometry connects update direction to editing robustness, compression diagnostics, and training-time intervention. Code is available in the https://github.com/Shwai-He/Transformer-Geometry."
}
